  "live_records": {
    "q-36983": {
      "dom_id": "step-q36983",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Which of the following is a basis of the eigenspace corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3?$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required basis is\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
